# Dates and times

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

These conventions apply across project languages. Coursework follows its
applicable course instructions.

## Dates and event timestamps

Use `YYYY-MM-DD` for calendar dates. Do not invent a time or timezone for a
date-only value.

Standardize application-generated event timestamps on UTC. Use timezone-aware
types where supported. When serialized as text, use an ISO 8601 timestamp with
a `T` separator and `Z` suffix, such as `2026-11-09T14:30:00Z`.

Use native temporal types where appropriate; these text conventions do not
require storing every date or timestamp as a string.

## Source time semantics

Preserve original source values in the raw stage under
[pipeline_io.mdc](pipeline_io.mdc).

Interpret source times using their documented timezone, time system, epoch,
units, and precision. Do not assume acquisition times or numeric time fields
are UTC timestamps.

Convert instants to UTC in working or final stages when the source semantics
support a valid conversion. Retain metadata needed to explain the interpretation.

Do not guess missing timezone information, silently resolve ambiguous local
times, or invent precision. Surface uncertainty and agree on its handling.

## Display and filenames

Local-time display is appropriate when useful to the user. Identify the timezone
and preserve the underlying UTC instant.

Compact UTC timestamps may be used in filenames. Make their UTC meaning clear
and do not rely on timestamps alone to guarantee unique output paths.

## Durations and measurements

Keep elapsed durations distinct from calendar dates and event timestamps.
State duration units explicitly in interfaces, metadata, and reported measurements.

Use a monotonic clock for elapsed runtime measurements. Do not calculate elapsed
runtime from civil-clock timestamps that can change during execution.
